WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:06.490
Ava: This is me, in a real Gmail, writing a real draft. Nobody is typing for me.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:16.750
Ava: First, a quick look at who I am. I'll keep it short.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:20.725
Ava: Hi, I'm Ava

00:00:20.725 --> 00:00:24.450
Ava: Give me an outcome, like entering an invoice and routing it for approval

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:28.175
Ava: I'll read how your company works, do the work in a real browser, and bring back proof that it's done

00:00:28.175 --> 00:00:31.900
Ava: Not just my word for it.

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:38.300
Ava: Before I touch anything, I read the company: policies, emails and three hundred past bills

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:44.700
Ava: Here, the procedure still says Raj approves design spend

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:51.100
Ava: But the CFO moved it to Ana in July, and eight of the nine bills since agree

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:57.500
Ava: When the same model ran without this step, it picked Raj, and the grader failed it.

00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:03.510
Ava: One request. Five company apps, your real Gmail, and your real Google Calendar.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:23.130
Ava: I read the invoice email, check its PDF in Drive, the latest bill and its vendor in Books, the approver's week on the company calendar, and the design channel. Every fact keeps its source.

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:28.900
Ava: Then a second tab. Gmail is a site we don't control, so the rules move into code. I can only write to the people you named, and Send is refused before it happens.

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:49.920
Ava: Then your calendar. I read your week, pick a free half hour, and hold it. No guests, ever.

00:01:53.970 --> 00:02:01.840
Ava: I don't mark my own work from memory. I read the draft and the event back from Google. Sixteen of sixteen.

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:05.900
Ava: And it's really there. Unsent, waiting for you.

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:25.750
Loki: And then there's me. Loki. I don't believe her report. I read the company's records myself.

00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:32.430
Ava: Exact checks first. Then a jury from two different model families reads the exhibits.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:43.760
Ava: Eleven charges. Eleven pass.

00:02:49.300 --> 00:03:00.532
Loki: Routed, you say? I just read Books myself. Approver: not requested. Status: draft. [Staged explanation of recorded recovery.]

00:03:00.532 --> 00:03:02.600
Ava: That is not what I saved. [Staged explanation of recorded recovery.]

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:14.132
Loki: Books agrees now. Thirteen of thirteen. Branches pruned. One timeline. [Staged explanation of recorded recovery.]

00:03:14.132 --> 00:03:16.772
Ava: Thank you for checking my work, Loki. [Staged explanation of recorded recovery.]

00:03:16.772 --> 00:03:21.772
Loki: Somebody has to. For the record, they broke it on purpose to test us. [Staged explanation of recorded recovery.]

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:30.500
Ava: Everything I do leaves a note: what I checked, what I'm unsure of, and where it came from.

00:03:38.030 --> 00:03:44.030
Ava: You can correct me. And a proven run can become a skill. A draft first, never trusted until it passes a rehearsal.

00:03:47.330 --> 00:03:51.310
Ava: Or just show me. Here's a colleague entering an invoice, once.

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:09.980
Ava: I watch, take notes step by step, and check every step against the real apps. Anything I can't confirm becomes a question.

00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:21.480
Ava: The lesson becomes a skill. I rehearse it in a sandbox company, and only then can you switch it on.

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:31.300
Ava: Everything here comes from real runs you can open, and some of them failed

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:33.900
Ava: That's in here too

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:36.500
Ava: When you're ready, give me a task

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:39.100
Ava: I'll meet you in the workspace.

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:42.940
Ava: Give me the outcome. I'll bring back the proof.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:45.150
Loki: And I'll check it.
